    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a decent high-level overview, but it feels a bit superficial for a legal discussion. Could you go deeper into the specific arguments for originalism? I'm interested in how they handle the \"well regulated Militia\" clause compared to the right of the people to keep and bear arms. Also, does the living constitutionalist side have a rigorous structural argument for why the text *must* change, or is it mostly just about societal shifts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism's core argument hinges on the text's literal meaning, with a focus on the \"well regulated Militia\" clause as the primary source of the Second Amendment's meaning. Originalists contend that the \"well regulated Militia\" clause was intended to ensure the continued existence of a citizen-soldier militia, and that the \"right of the people to keep and bear arms\" was meant to be a means to achieve this goal. This reading is often tied to the historical context of the late 18th century, when the Second Amendment was ratified, and the idea that the Founding Fathers were concerned with the preservation of a unified national defense.\n\nIn contrast, living constitutionalists argue that the text's meaning has evolved over time, and that the \"right of the people to keep and bear arms\" is a fundamental right that has taken on a life of its own, separate from its original purpose. They often point to the absence of any explicit reference to a militia in the text, and argue that the Founding Fathers' intentions were not fixed or static, but rather subject to the changing needs and values of the American people.",
      "source": "target",
